{
  "abstract": "Complete and unconditional. A Lean 4 formalization of G. Cassese, \"A solution to Morrey's problem in R^{2 x m}\" (arXiv:2608.03488v3, 2026): rank-one convex integrands on 2 x m matrices, m >= 3, that are nowhere quasiconvex, so that with Šverák's theorem only 2 x 2 remains open; and p-homogeneous examples answering Kirchheim and Kristensen's homogeneous problem negatively.\n\nEvery result that the paper proves is proved, with no hypothesis beyond the paper's: Theorem 1.2 (every p > 1, 2 x m for m large, by the paper's UMD route, and a second time by its elementary route), Theorem 1.3 (a quartic on 2 x 3, and p near 4), Corollary 5.3 (its transpose on 3 x 2), the consequences that only 2 x 2 remains, Theorem 1.4 (d x d, every p, and p = 4 for d >= 588), Theorem 1.5 (4 x 2, every p != 2) and Corollary 1.6. The results of other works that the proofs use are proved too, in their sources' terms: Burkholder's inequalities and functions, UMD theory for L^p and the Schatten classes, decoupling, the Kahane-Khintchine and non-commutative Khintchine inequalities, transference for homogeneous multipliers, Dragičević's lower bound for the square of the Beurling-Ahlfors transform, Dacorogna's formula for the quasiconvex envelope, Kristensen's smoothing and the textbook facts of the calculus of variations, by the arguments of their sources (named substitutes stand for a few steps that a source takes from a work that was not read, only recalls, or states without proof: see fidelity). They come from three Lake packages written for this project (lean4-harmonic-analysis, lean4-banach-probability, lean4-calculus-of-variations). The repository formalizes other papers on Morrey's problem too, each its own submission.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "The-Anh Vu-Le"
    }
  ],
